+## Design notes
+
+- `store::history` imports `crate::undo::candidates::read_action`, so a store module depends on the undo module. It works and stays within the crate. `action_at_head` could read the record through `layout::locate` and `read_toml` directly; this is cosmetic.
+- `last_touches` returns no `ScanReport`. A path missing from the map can mean "never touched" or "touched before the cap", and `path_last_touched: null` cannot tell the two apart. §2.3 does not require the distinction for this column.
+- `Sort::TOPOLOGICAL` in `commits()` is checked against the vendored libgit2 1.9.7 source (libgit2-sys 0.18.8, revwalk.c). `git_revwalk_sorting` sets `walk->limited = 1` for any sort other than NONE, and `prepare_walk` then runs `limit_list` over the whole reachable first-parent chain before it yields the first id. So every `walk`/`last_touches` call costs O(full history) commit parses, whatever the cap. The cost is one commit-header parse per commit, not a tree diff, and output is unaffected. The writer only ever makes single-parent commits, so `Sort::NONE` with `simplify_first_parent` would give the same newest-first order. This compounds with `inspect::loaded::clone_view`, which builds a fresh walker per stale clone; that call pattern belongs to the inspect cluster.
+
+## Concerns
+
+- `HistoryWalker::commits` uses `Sort::TOPOLOGICAL`, which makes libgit2 walk the whole first-parent chain before yielding the first commit. The spec's §2.4 cost model ("one commit read and one tree diff per scanned commit", bounded by `--max-scan`) is therefore not strictly met for long histories. The output is correct.
+- `last_touches` early exit compares the number of distinct paths found with the input length. Duplicate input paths therefore scan to the cap. This affects performance only.
+
